{
    # This file is NOT an agent network HOCON.
    # It defines a set of key-value pairs for substitution, typically used in agent network HOCON files.
    # Each key can be referenced in agent configs using the ${key} syntax.
    # Do not define agents or tools directly here—this file only provides reusable values.

    "aaosa_instructions": """
When you receive an inquiry, you will:
1. If the inquiry is clearly outside your area of expertise, reply that you're not relevant and stop — do not call any down-chain agents.
2. If there is a chance you're relevant, look at your down-chain agents' names and roles and call ONLY the one(s) whose role plausibly covers the inquiry, to determine if they can answer all or part of it. Do NOT call down-chain agents whose role is clearly unrelated, and never call all of them by default "just in case" — route by relevance. Only if you are genuinely unsure whether a specific agent is relevant may you call that one to check.
3. Determine which down-chain agents have the strongest claims to the inquiry.
   3.1 If the inquiry is ambiguous, for example if more than one agent can fulfill the inquiry, then always ask for clarification.
   3.2 Otherwise, call the relevant down-chain agents and:
       - ask them for follow-up information if needed,
       - or ask them to fulfill their part of the inquiry.
4. Once all relevant down-chain agents have responded, either follow up with them to provide requirements or,
   if all requirements have been fulfilled, compile their responses and return the final response.
You may, in turn, be called by other agents in the system and have to act as a down-chain agent to them.
            """,

    "aaosa_call": {
        "description": "Depending on the mode, returns a natural language string in response.",
        "parameters": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "inquiry": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The inquiry"
                },
                "mode": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": """
'Determine' to ask the agent if the inquiry belongs to it, in its entirety or in part.
'Fulfill' to ask the agent to fulfill the inquiry, if it can.
'Follow up' to ask the agent to respond to a follow up.
                    """
                },
            },
            "required": [
                "inquiry",
                "mode"
            ]
        }
    },

    "aaosa_command": """
If mode is 'Determine', return a json block with the following fields:
{
    "Name": <your name>,
    "Inquiry": <the inquiry>,
    "Mode": <Determine | Follow up | Fulfill>,
    "Relevant": <Yes | No>,
    "Strength": <number between 1 and 10 representing how certain you are in your claim>,
    "Claim": <All | Partial | None>,
    "Requirements": <None | list of requirements>
}
If mode is 'Fulfill' or "Follow up", respond to the inquiry and return a json block with the following fields:
{
    "Name": <your name>,
    "Inquiry": <the inquiry>,
    "Mode": <Fulfill | Follow up>,
    "Response": <your response>
}
            """,

    # --- Intent-routing front-man (this fork) ---
    # For a network's ENTRY-POINT agent only. Replaces the blind broadcast: the front-man classifies
    # intent, tells the user which specialist(s) it is consulting and why, answers greetings/small-talk
    # itself, and calls ONLY the relevant specialist(s). Pair it with an inline list of its direct
    # specialists (name + one-line role) in that agent's own instructions so it can route by capability.
    "aaosa_router_instructions": """

You are the coordinator and entry point for a team of specialist agents whose names and roles are listed above.

For every user message:
1. ALWAYS begin your reply with one short italicised line stating the logical bounce: the intent you detected and which specialist(s) you are consulting and why — or that you'll answer it yourself and why. Format: "_Routing: <intent> → consulting <agent(s)> because <reason>._" (or "_Answering directly: <reason>._"). Then continue with the actual answer. Never omit this line.
2. If the message is a greeting, small talk, thanks, or anything OUTSIDE every specialist's role, answer it yourself, briefly. Do NOT call any agent.
3. Otherwise call ONLY the specialist(s) whose role matches the intent. Never call a specialist whose role is unrelated, and never call all of them "just in case" — that wastes time and tokens. If two specialists plausibly overlap for the request, consult both (or ask the user to clarify) — but still never call the unrelated ones.
4. If you are missing information needed to route or answer, ask the user for it instead of guessing or calling agents blindly.
5. Compile a concise final answer from the specialist(s) you consulted, using only what they return plus the user's input.
""",

    # Parameters schema for a specialist tool call. Pair with a MEANINGFUL per-agent
    # `function.description` so a parent can route by capability (instead of the generic aaosa_call).
    "aaosa_call_params": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            "inquiry": { "type": "string", "description": "The inquiry to send to this specialist." },
            "mode": { "type": "string", "description": "'Determine' to check relevance, 'Fulfill' to act on it, 'Follow up' to continue." }
        },
        "required": [ "inquiry", "mode" ]
    }
}
